== Release ==
=== Theatrical ===
''Clayface'' premiered at the Hollywood Legion Theater in Los Angeles on October 13, 2026,<ref name="LAPremiere" /> and was released theatrically by [[Warner Bros. Pictures]] in the United States on October 23.<ref name="Oct2026Date" /> It was originally scheduled for release on September 11, 2026,<ref name="Sep2026Date" /> but was pushed back to the October date in February 2026 to take advantage of the [[Halloween]] period.<ref name="Oct2026DelayReason" /> It is part of the DCU's Chapter One: Gods and Monsters.<ref name="DCUSlateFeb2025Deadline" />

=== Home media ===
''Clayface'' is set to be released on [[Digital distribution|digital download]] on December 8, 2026.<ref name="DigitalRelease" />

== Reception ==
=== Box office ===
{{As of|2026|11|26|df=US}}, ''Clayface'' has grossed $66.6&nbsp;million in the United States and Canada, and $60&nbsp;million in other territories, for a worldwide total of $126.6&nbsp;million.<ref name="NUM" /><ref name="BOM" />

''Clayface'' was projected to gross $25–35&nbsp;million in its opening weekend in the United States and Canada, and $20–25&nbsp;million internationally, for a worldwide opening of $45–55&nbsp;million.<ref name="FirstWeekendProjection" /> It earned $24.5&nbsp;million in its opening weekend in the United States and Canada, with $10.4&nbsp;million on Friday (including previews), and reached $43&nbsp;million worldwide. It attracted 73% of viewers aged 18–34, with Hispanic and Latino audiences accounting for 35% of it.<ref name="DeadlineBO1stWknd" /> In its second weekend, with a 57% drop, the film earned $10.4&nbsp;million in the United States and Canada, taking its total to $44&nbsp;million, and reached $83&nbsp;million worldwide.<ref name="DeadlineBO2stWknd" /> In its third weekend, ''Clayface'' grossed $6.675&nbsp;million in the United States and Canada, a 39% drop from the previous weekend.<ref name="DeadlineBO3stWknd" />

=== Critical response ===
{{Metacritic breakdown|positive=24|mixed=19|negative=3}}
''Clayface'' received generally positive reviews from critics, with praise for Rhys Harries's performance, the body horror, and the use of practical effects, but some criticism for its formulaic script, visual effects, and the depiction of [[Gotham City]].<ref name="ReviewRoundupTHR" /><ref name="ReviewRoundupRT" /> {{RT prose|78|6.6|218|Carving out a unique identity for itself in the DCU while closely resembling a litany of body horror classics, ''Clayface'' takes time to get going until it eventually morphs into an icky and satisfying revenge thriller.|ref=yes|access-date=November 4, 2026}} [[Metacritic]], which uses a [[Weighted arithmetic mean|weighted average]], assigned the film a score of 58 out of 100, based on 46 critics, indicating "mixed or average" reviews.<ref name="Metacritic">{{Cite Metacritic |id=clayface |type=m |title=Clayface |access-date=October 27, 2026}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Audiences polled by [[CinemaScore]] gave the film an average grade of "A−" on an A+ to F scale,<ref name="DeadlineBO1stWknd" /> while 68% of those surveyed by [[PostTrak]] said they would definitely recommend the film.<ref name="DeadlineBO2stWknd" />

[[Frank Scheck]] of ''[[The Hollywood Reporter]]'' praised the film as "a big bet that pays off", highlighting [[Tom Rhys Harries]]' performance and the portrayal of Matt Hagen's physical and emotional deterioration. Scheck also praised [[James Watkins (director)|James Watkins]] for his direction of the body horror sequences and the use of practical effects, and highlighted the psychological depth and emotional resonance of the film. He also compared it to the works of body horror filmmakers [[David Cronenberg]] and [[Sam Raimi]].<ref name="ReviewTHR" /> Guy Lodge of ''[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]'' praised ''Clayface'' as a refreshingly, grotesque body-horror film that exceeds expectations without losing its B-movie spirit, highlighting Watkins' direction, Rhys Harries' anguished performance and the film's grisly practical effects. [[Hossein Amini]]'s screenplay was commended for its sharp dialogue and sly humour, while [[Naomi Ackie]]'s performance and the imaginative casting added depth to the characters.<ref name="VarietyReview" /> Glenn Garner for ''[[Deadline Hollywood]]'' praised the graphic body horror and jump scares, and the chemistry between Rhys Harries and Ackie, which adds a dark romantic element to the film. Garner also praised its exploration of Hollywood's obsession with beauty, police corruption and medical companies prioritising cosmetic procedures over life-saving research, without becoming preachy.<ref name="DeadlineReview" />

[[Amy Nicholson]] from the ''[[Los Angeles Times]]'' praised the inventive body-horror sequences but criticised its slow pacing, lack of originality and weak characterisation. Nicholson also faulted the screenplay for failing to develop Matt's moral complexity and the romance between Rhys Harries and Ackie, arguing that the film lacks emotional impact.<ref name="LATReview" /> Brian Tallarico from ''[[RogerEbert.com]]'' gave the film a one-and-a-half-star-out-of-four review. While noting that the film "ultimately gets to a few memorable set pieces," Tallarico felt that Rhys Harries and Watkins lacked a clear vision for approaching Matt's journey, criticized the performance as inconsistent, and considered the film's vision of [[Gotham City]] dull.<ref name="RogerEbert.comReview">{{Cite web |last=Tallarico |first=Brian |title=Clayface review: DC blockbuster never takes an interesting shape |url=https://www.rogerebert.com/reviews/clayface-dc-movie-review-2026 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261024075859/https://www.rogerebert.com/reviews/clayface-dc-movie-review-2026 |archive-date=October 24, 2026 |access-date=October 26, 2026 |website=[[RogerEbert.com]]}}</ref>

